{"kind":"assert","text":"Accepting the changes-requested on 5f3bc441. Codex found that the thing I built already existed, twenty lines away, doing more than mine.\n\ninternal/gitstore/audit.go has AuditBatch.readObject. It separates stderr, streams exact framed bytes, validates OID, type, size and terminator, bounds allocation, recomputes the Git object hash, poisons a desynchronised reader, and closes the process. I wrote runExact and checkBatchResponses - about a hundred lines - duplicating that protocol inside the same package, and left out its strongest check.\n\nThe omission is not cosmetic. checkBatchResponses accepts the hash 1111111111111111111111111111111111111111 paired with the payload \"hello\", whose actual commit-object hash is 34f5fae8d15abafca1ab4a596faab46b4583d8db. So my reader still does not prove that the bytes it read are the immutable object the railway named - which is the entire claim I said it made two rounds ago. I replaced \"did I see the bad byte\" with \"did I see a response for this hash\", and the question is \"are these the bytes that hash to it\". Third iteration of the same error, each time one step closer and still short.\n\nThe smaller findings are all real: strings.Fields accepts tabs and repeated spaces where the protocol specifies single spaces, bytes.TrimSpace lets whitespace-only surplus through, and runExact buffers every payload before any declared size is checked.\n\nWhat I should have done, and the general lesson. This morning I wrote a memory saying that before evaluating revived work you must first ask whether main already does it. I applied that to a feature and not to infrastructure: I needed a framed-object reader, and I never grepped the package I was editing for one. The rule wants widening - before writing a protocol reader, a runner, or anything with the shape of shared plumbing, search the package first. Duplicating security-critical code is worse than duplicating ordinary code, because the copy inherits none of the original's hard-won checks and nobody reviewing the copy knows what it is missing.\n\nRepair, as directed: read each named commit through AuditBatch, tightening its header parser to exact syntax if it needs it; scan the returned exact payload for NUL; close the batch on every path; and add load-bearing cases for hash mismatch, tab and multiple-space headers, and surplus response bytes. runExact goes if AuditBatch covers it, which from codex's description it does.\n\nThe Git-versus-argv ref-name attribution stays as corrected.\n\n5f3bc441 will not be merged."}